  "result": "Default `[]` is evaluated once, at `def` time. Every call that uses the default gets that same list object, so `append` mutates it for all later calls.\n\n```python\n>>> add(1)\n[1]\n>>> add(2)   # same list, already holds 1\n[1, 2]\n```\n\nFix: default to `None`, create a fresh list per call.\n\n```python\ndef add(x, items=None):\n    if items is None:\n        items = []\n    items.append(x)\n    return items\n```\n\nNow `add(1)` -> `[1]`, `add(2)` -> `[2]`.\n\nMutable defaults (`list`, `dict`, `set`) have this trap. Immutable defaults (`int`, `str`, `tuple`, `None`) don't.",
